7fd478e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<4\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12cfbabe-7848-5378-9c22-fd0a0d819de6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542118 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\cdot 2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\cdot 2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<4\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<4\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22f420b4-6e58-565d-a3ee-d0991eb0a624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542128 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{120}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={60}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={210}^{∘}$ and $x={240}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={30}^{∘},{60}^{∘},{210}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={60}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={240}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=60+240=300.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2edac9cd-9abe-5210-bf19-134c059eeaf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542129 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58a09865-e871-55ba-9b1a-dcc38d05fe6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542148 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-{60}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & x-{60}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={30}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={150}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=90\\cdot 210=18\\,900.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=0$ for $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values?\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}${}^{∘}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6785b9b0-7bde-5da0-a762-84192fbca0cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542152 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{60}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x-{60}^{∘} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{60}^{∘}$ and $x=-{45}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘},-{45}^{∘},{30}^{∘},{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\nx={-60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={45}^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{30}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b0a40ef-6c46-57c1-945d-c08da744533d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542156 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{30}^{∘} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{30}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-{30}^{∘}$ and $x={90}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={150}^{∘}$  and $x={270}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={330}^{∘}$  and $x={450}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘},{150}^{∘},{270}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={150}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={270}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=150\\cdot 270=40\\,500.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9a4a2f3-242d-5c49-b478-bafd32239530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542183 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(24n+17)\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(24n+25)\\pi }{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{25\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{48}$ (too small) and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{49\\pi }{48}$ (too big)\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\frac{41\\pi }{48}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afef3ba2-5b32-54ef-ad36-e24901c18733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542192 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =2\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi (4n+3)}{6} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2(n+1)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ (too big)\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ (too small) and $x=0.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ in **ascending order**, what are the values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}?$ Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6f8b9f9-43bb-5fdf-8d90-07de7dc4a372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542195 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+3)\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are: \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9773b0d-55ae-555a-a7e8-48475fa96d55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542200 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(2+3n)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\pi $ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd3c6406-7b28-59e8-9519-0dcd8ba0850a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542201 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{45}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{135}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={15}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={165}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={255}^{∘}$ and $x={285}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘},{45}^{∘},{135}^{∘},{165}^{∘},{255}^{∘},{285}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={45}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={165}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=45+165=210.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1069 "q-280018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘})-1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values?\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}${}^{∘}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6e2414a-60ee-52e9-b582-38de6779c295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542204 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘})-1 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘}) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{4}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x-{4}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={34}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={154}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={17}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={77}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={17}^{∘}$ and $x={77}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={197}^{∘}$ and $x={257}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={17}^{∘},{77}^{∘},{197}^{∘},{257}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={17}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={77}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={197}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={257}^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-279986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efa055ae-f23c-562f-bd3a-51db3280b097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186542223 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
